    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d853">

                <pb facs="0894" n="782" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg782"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 2491/54–56.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Minister
                    Squiers.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1909-01-30"><hi rendition="#italic">January 30,
                        1909</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 153.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to department’s instruction to
                    you of the 23d instant, concerning the attitude of the United States regarding
                    the boundary controversy between Panama and Costa Rica, I have to say that the
                    present attitude of this Government is the logical consequence of its
                    instruction to Minister Magoon of April 16, 1906,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d853-f1"
                    /> a copy of which is inclosed, not only for your information, but for
                    presentation to the Panaman minister of foreign affairs, in order to show not
                    only the constant attitude of the United States, but the necessity of such
                    attitude for the protection of the property interests of American citizens in
                    the disputed territory.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
